<h1>Engineering judgement first.<br>AI as a fast, checkable collaborator.</h1>
<p class="lead">Structural design, plastic and yield-line theory, plate mechanics, and a materials-testing thesis. This page shows the work, and the way I use AI to get through it quicker without handing over responsibility for the answer.</p>

<h2>Selected work</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><span class="tag">Eurocode 2</span><span class="tag">BS 5400</span><span class="tag">Moment distribution</span>
<h3>Single-cell box culvert</h3>
<p>3.0 m × 2.0 m section, 600 mm fill, HA traffic loading with KEL, saturated-soil design case. Full chain: load takedown, frame analysis, flexure, shear, and crack width.</p>
<div class="stats"><div class="stat"><b>71 kNm</b><span>top slab Mmax</span></div><div class="stat"><b>T20-150</b><span>top steel</span></div><div class="stat"><b>0.23 mm</b><span>wall crack width (limit 0.25)</span></div></div></div>

<div class="card"><span class="tag">Eurocode 2</span><span class="tag">Indeterminate frame</span><span class="tag">SLS</span>
<h3>Twin-cell box culvert</h3>
<p>Two 2.4 m cells, 5.7 m overall. Middle wall carries axial load only; outer walls take lateral earth and water pressure. Eleven-cycle moment distribution at ultimate and service.</p>
<div class="stats"><div class="stat"><b>113 kNm</b><span>bottom slab support</span></div><div class="stat"><b>T25-125</b><span>bottom steel</span></div><div class="stat"><b>402 kN/m</b><span>middle wall axial</span></div></div></div>

<div class="card"><span class="tag">Research</span><span class="tag">Lab testing</span><span class="tag">BS 5628</span>
<h3>Cement-clay mortar for block joining</h3>
<p>Final-year project with Martin Kyalo Ndunda. Five mixes (0–20% cement) tested for flow, setting time and 7/14/28-day cube strength, after characterising the clay (LL 45.02%, PL 26.31%, PI 18.71%).</p>
<div class="stats"><div class="stat"><b>20%</b><span>best cement ratio</span></div><div class="stat"><b>95.2%</b><span>flow, Mix E</span></div><div class="stat"><b>3.5 N/mm²</b><span>peak strength</span></div></div></div>

<div class="card"><span class="tag">Theory</span><span class="tag">Study notes</span>
<h3>Advanced structural analysis</h3>
<p>Worked notes on plastic analysis (hinges, shape factor, collapse mechanisms for beams and portals), yield-line analysis of one-way and two-way slabs, and elastic plates and shells (circular plates, clamped vs simply supported).</p>
<p style="margin:0;color:var(--mute);font-size:14px">These give me the collapse-load and plate formulas I use to sanity-check numerical output.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Single cell vs twin cell: one method, two answers</h2>
<div class="card scroll"><table>
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Single cell</th><th>Twin cell</th></tr>
<tr><td>Top slab design moment</td><td>71.18 kNm (span)</td><td>93.92 kNm (support C)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Top slab steel</td><td>T20-150 (2094 mm²/m)</td><td>T25-175 (2805 mm²/m)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bottom slab design moment</td><td>91.53 kNm (span)</td><td>113.08 kNm (support D)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bottom slab steel</td><td>T25-175 (2805 mm²/m)</td><td>T25-125 (3927 mm²/m)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Outer wall steel (near face)</td><td>T12-150</td><td>T12-125</td></tr>
<tr><td>Slab shear utilisation</td><td>171.7 / 177.6 kN (97%)</td><td>190.2 / 195.0 kN (98%)</td></tr>
