<p class="note">Shear in both top slabs sits at about 97–98% of V<sub>Rd,c</sub>. That is a pass, but the margin is thin, and it is the first thing I would revisit if loads or cover change.</p>

<h2>How I use AI</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><b>Set up the problem myself.</b> I choose the code, the load cases, the structural model and the assumptions (soil saturated, 1 m strip, fixed-end frame). AI never decides these for me.</li>
<li><b>Let AI do the repetitive arithmetic.</b> Load takedowns, moment-distribution cycles, neutral-axis quadratics and reinforcement tables are laid out as calculation sheets with references beside each line, so every number can be traced.</li>
<li><b>Check it against theory.</b> Reactions must sum to the applied load, frame moments must balance at joints, and results get compared with the closed-form cases from my plastic-analysis and plate notes.</li>
<li><b>Interrogate the output.</b> I ask the AI to explain each clause used, challenge my reinforcement choices, and flag anything close to a limit (like the crack width of 0.23 mm against 0.25 mm).</li>
<li><b>Own the sign-off.</b> Drawings, details and final decisions are mine. The AI speeds up the working, and I am accountable for the result.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Where AI helps, and where it doesn’t</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Good use</h3><p>Drafting calculation sheets, cross-checking hand results, explaining code clauses, structuring a report, and turning lecture notes into worked examples.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Not trusted blindly</h3><p>Code clause numbers, partial factors, site-specific loading and anything that goes to construction. These get verified against the standard itself.</p></div>
